---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="VMTR-01 Privacy Policy — Vireal" description="How VMTR-01 handles audio recordings, device permissions, analytics, diagnostics, retention, and privacy requests.">
  <div class="prose">
    <p class="eyebrow"><a href="/vmtr01/">VMTR-01</a> / Privacy</p>
    <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
    <p class="meta">Effective and last updated: October 18, 2026</p>

    <p>
      VMTR-01 — Multitrack Recorder (Android package <code>com.vireal.vmtr01</code>) is developed by
      Vireal. This policy describes information VMTR-01 accesses on your device and information
      processed by our service providers when you use the app. It applies to the Android app,
      including its production release.
    </p>

    <h2>What the app does with your audio</h2>
    <p>
      VMTR-01 records audio received from compatible USB audio interfaces, lets you monitor and play
      recordings, and lets you export or share files. Audio samples and recording files are processed
      on your Android device. The app does not intentionally upload audio recordings to Vireal,
      Amplitude, or Sentry. If you choose to share a recording using Android's share sheet, the
      destination app or service you select receives the content under its own privacy practices.
    </p>
    <p>
      Recordings can be stored in shared media storage (including the <code>Music/VMTR-01/</code>
      location) and may remain accessible to other apps with appropriate access. VMTR-01 also
      processes local recording information such as file names, durations, track counts, and file
      sizes to display and manage your recording library. We do not operate a cloud recording library.
    </p>

    <h2>Permissions and device access</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Microphone/audio recording:</strong> Android's <code>RECORD_AUDIO</code> permission supports the recording function. The app captures audio for the recording and monitoring features you use; it does not use your recordings for advertising.</li>
      <li><strong>USB device access:</strong> VMTR-01 discovers compatible attached USB audio devices and requests USB access so it can record or monitor their audio.</li>
      <li><strong>Audio/media library:</strong> <code>READ_MEDIA_AUDIO</code> (or <code>READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE</code> on older Android versions) may be requested to find or reopen recordings in shared storage, including after local app data has been cleared.</li>
      <li><strong>Notifications:</strong> <code>POST_NOTIFICATIONS</code> may be requested to show recording-related notifications. Recording and playback may also use Android foreground services.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      You can manage app permissions in Android Settings. Denying a permission may prevent the
      corresponding feature from working.
    </p>

    <h2>Usage analytics (Amplitude)</h2>
    <p>
      Production versions of VMTR-01 use <a href="https://amplitude.com/privacy">Amplitude</a>
      to understand feature usage, device compatibility, and recording reliability. The app sends
      app-open and automatic session, application lifecycle, and screen-view events. It also sends
      events for USB device connection, recording start, completion, failure, deletion, and sharing.
      Event properties can include USB device name/model, channel count, sample rate, recording
      duration, recording file size, failure category, elapsed time, and an error-message string.
      The error-message field could contain additional diagnostic text depending on the failure.
    </p>
    <p>
      Amplitude may also process automatically collected technical information, including app and
      OS versions, device manufacturer/model, platform and language, event times, session data, and
      a pseudonymous SDK-generated device/installation identifier. We do not require a user
      account, and the app does not intentionally set an account identity for analytics.
      The SDK is configured not to collect Android advertising ID, App Set ID, carrier, IP address
      as an analytics property, or city, region, country, and precise coordinates. However, an IP
      address is necessarily processed by network services when data is transmitted and provider
      infrastructure may use it for routing, security, or other service operations. These SDK
      settings do not remove data that a provider may already hold.
    </p>
    <p>
      Analytics runs automatically in the production build; VMTR-01 currently does not provide an
      in-app analytics opt-out switch. QA builds have app analytics disabled.
    </p>

    <h2>Crash and error reporting (Sentry)</h2>
    <p>
      We use <a href="https://sentry.io/privacy/">Sentry</a> to investigate crashes and handled
      errors. Reports can include exception types, sanitized error messages, stack traces,
      operation categories, limited numeric diagnostics, timestamps, app release/environment,
      Android version, and device manufacturer/model. Sentry may also process network metadata
      needed to receive and protect reports, including the connecting IP address.
    </p>
    <p>
      VMTR-01 disables Sentry's default personal-information submission, screenshots, view
      hierarchy attachments, and user-interaction breadcrumbs/tracing. It removes user and request
      objects, device names and IDs, and most unapproved diagnostic extras before sending events;
      known file paths, content URIs, and credential-like values in exception messages are redacted.
      These measures reduce exposure but cannot guarantee that arbitrary third-party exception
      messages contain no personal information.
    </p>

    <h2>Why information is processed and who receives it</h2>
    <p>
      We use technical analytics to understand which features are used and which USB configurations
      work, and diagnostic reports to identify failures and improve app stability. Amplitude
      processes analytics data and Sentry processes diagnostic data on our behalf. They are
      separate service providers, and their infrastructure may process information in other
      countries. We do not intentionally sell recordings or personal data, and we do not use
      analytics or diagnostic information for targeted advertising.
    </p>

    <h2>Security</h2>
    <p>
      Analytics and diagnostic data are transmitted to the relevant providers using their SDKs
      and HTTPS/TLS network connections. Access to provider dashboards is restricted to
      authorized project administrators. VMTR-01 applies client-side minimization and redaction
      to Sentry reports. Files stored in shared Android media storage are protected according to
      Android's storage access rules and your device's security settings, not by end-to-end
      encryption supplied by VMTR-01.
    </p>

    <h2>Retention and deletion</h2>
    <p>
      Local recordings remain on your device until you delete them, subject to your device's own
      storage, backup, and file-management behavior. You can delete recordings using the app
      where supported or Android file-management tools. Removing the app or clearing its data
      does not necessarily delete recordings previously written to shared storage.
      App preferences and temporary local data may be removed when app data is cleared or the app
      is uninstalled; Android backup or device-transfer features may retain eligible app data
      according to your device and account settings.
    </p>
    <p>
      Amplitude and Sentry retain telemetry under their configured project retention settings and
      applicable service policies; it is not deleted merely because you uninstall VMTR-01.
      To request information about, or deletion of, telemetry associated with your installation,
      contact us using the method below. We may ask for information needed to locate matching
      records; because analytics identifiers are pseudonymous and we do not have user accounts,
      we may not always be able to match an individual request to existing telemetry.
      We will review and handle verifiable requests as required by applicable law.
    </p>

    <h2>Children</h2>
    <p>
      VMTR-01 is a technical audio-recording tool and is not designed or marketed for children.
      We do not knowingly request children's personal information.
    </p>

    <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
    <p>
      We may revise this policy when the app or its data practices change. We will update the
      effective date on this page; any additional notice required by law will be provided.
    </p>

    <h2>Developer and privacy contact</h2>
    <p>
      Developer: <strong>Vireal</strong> (VMTR-01). To ask a privacy question or submit an access
      or deletion request, <a href="https://github.com/vireal01/vireal.dev/issues/new">open a
      request in the vireal.dev GitHub repository</a>. Do not post sensitive personal information
      or audio recordings in a public issue. You may also use the contact options available on
      the <a href="https://github.com/vireal01">developer's GitHub profile</a>.
    </p>
    <p><a href="/vmtr01/">← Back to VMTR-01</a></p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
